    title: 'The Great Barrier Reef and Coral Bleaching',
    part: 2,
    level: 'B2',
    passage: `Stretching more than 2,300 kilometers along the northeast coast of Australia, the Great Barrier Reef is the largest living structure on the planet. Composed of nearly 3,000 individual reef systems and hundreds of tropical islands, it can even be seen from outer space. This extraordinary underwater ecosystem is home to thousands of marine species, including clownfish, sea turtles, manta rays, and more than 400 varieties of coral.

Despite its grandeur, the Great Barrier Reef is facing an existential crisis caused by climate change. Corals are marine invertebrates that rely on a delicate symbiotic relationship with microscopic algae called zooxanthellae. These algae reside within the coral polyps' tissues, producing essential nutrients through photosynthesis and lending corals their vibrant pink, purple, and green pigments. In exchange, the coral polyp provides the algae with a protected environment and the compounds needed for photosynthesis.

When seawater temperatures rise even 1 to 2 degrees Celsius above the historical summer maximum, corals undergo physiological thermal stress. In response, they expel the zooxanthellae from their tissues. Without the colorful algae, the transparent coral tissue reveals the stark white calcium carbonate skeleton underneath—a phenomenon known as coral bleaching. While bleached corals are not immediately dead, they are deprived of their primary energy source, rendering them vulnerable to starvation and lethal disease.

Mass bleaching events have occurred with alarming frequency over the past decade. Severe ocean heatwaves in 2016, 2017, 2020, and 2022 affected massive swathes of the northern and central sections of the reef. Scientists estimate that the 2016 bleaching event alone killed nearly 30 percent of the shallow-water corals on the Great Barrier Reef.

The degradation of coral reefs has profound ecological and economic consequences. Reefs act as natural storm barriers, dissipating up to 97 percent of wave energy and protecting coastal communities from severe erosion and cyclones. Economically, the Great Barrier Reef contributes more than 6 billion Australian dollars annually to the national economy and supports around 64,000 jobs, primarily through ecotourism and commercial fisheries. Without urgent international action to curb greenhouse gas emissions, marine biologists warn that these magnificent underwater wonders could vanish by mid-century.`,
